module

public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic
public import Mathlib.Topology.Category.TopCat.Limits.Basic

/-!
# Two applications of the criterion for representability

Stacks 0AHP records the two standard uses of the representability criterion of Stacks 0AHN: it
produces free objects, and it produces colimits once limits are known to exist.

For the free group on a set `E` one applies the criterion to `F : Groups ⥤ Sets`,
`G ↦ Map(E, G)`, which commutes with limits and satisfies the solution set condition because a
map `E → G` factors through the subgroup generated by its image, of cardinality at most
`max ℵ₀ |E|`. The criterion then yields a group `F_E` with `Mor_{Groups}(F_E, G) = Map(E, G)`,
and the identity of `F_E` corresponds to a map `E → F_E` whose image generates `F_E`.

For colimits of topological spaces one applies the criterion to `F : Top ⥤ Sets`,
`Y ↦ lim_𝓘 Mor_{Top}(X_i, Y)`, using that `Top` has limits (Stacks 08ZT); a space representing
`F` is precisely a colimit of the diagram `i ↦ X_i`.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.bijective_comp_freeGroup_of` and
  `CategoryTheory.closure_range_freeGroup_of`, Stacks 0AHP: the free group on `E` represents
  `G ↦ Map(E, G)`, and it is generated by the image of `E`;
* `CategoryTheory.colimitHomEquiv`, Stacks 0AHP: a colimit of a diagram of topological spaces is
  exactly a space representing `Y ↦ lim_𝓘 Mor_{Top}(X_i, Y)`, the limit in question being the set
  of families `(φ_i : X_i ⟶ Y)` compatible with the transition maps, i.e. of morphisms of
  diagrams `X ⟶ const Y`.

## Implementation notes

Only the *outcomes* of the two applications are stated here; the criterion itself is Stacks 0AHN
(`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Representable.SolutionSet`), and both outcomes are available in
Mathlib in the shape the source predicts — `CategoryTheory.Limits.HasColimit` for `TopCat`
and `FreeGroup.lift` for groups.
The point of the remark is that these two objects *are* the representing objects of the two
functors, so that is what is proved: a bijection between morphisms out of the candidate and the
value of the functor.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace CategoryTheory

open Limits

universe v u

/-- **Stacks 0AHP**, the free group on a set.
The free group on `E` represents the functor `G ↦ Map(E, G)` on groups: composing a group
homomorphism `F_E → G` with the canonical map `FreeGroup.of : E → F_E` is a bijection from
`Mor_{Groups}(F_E, G)` to `Map(E, G)`. -/
@[stacks 0AHP "the free group on a set"]
theorem bijective_comp_freeGroup_of (E : Type u) (G : Type v) [Group G] :
    Function.Bijective fun f : FreeGroup E →* G => (f : FreeGroup E → G) ∘ FreeGroup.of :=
  FreeGroup.lift.symm.bijective

/-- **Stacks 0AHP**, the free group on a set: the map `E → F_E` corresponding to the identity of
`F_E` generates `F_E`, so no relations have been imposed. -/
@[stacks 0AHP "the free group on a set"]
theorem closure_range_freeGroup_of (E : Type u) :
    Subgroup.closure (Set.range (FreeGroup.of : E → FreeGroup E)) = ⊤ :=
  FreeGroup.closure_range_of E

/-- **Stacks 0AHP**, colimits of topological spaces.
For a diagram `X : 𝓘 ⥤ Top` of topological spaces, a morphism out of the colimit of `X` is the
same thing as an element of `lim_𝓘 Mor_{Top}(X_i, Y)`, i.e. a family of morphisms
`φ_i : X_i ⟶ Y` compatible with the transition maps of the diagram. So the colimit — which exists
because `Top` has limits and the criterion of Stacks 0AHN applies — is precisely a space
representing the functor `Y ↦ lim_𝓘 Mor_{Top}(X_i, Y)` of the source. -/
@[stacks 0AHP "colimits of topological spaces"]
noncomputable def colimitHomEquiv {𝓘 : Type u} [SmallCategory 𝓘] (X : 𝓘 ⥤ TopCat.{u})
    (Y : TopCat.{u}) :
    (colimit X ⟶ Y) ≃ (X ⟶ (Functor.const 𝓘).obj Y) where
  toFun f := (colimit.cocone X).ι ≫ (Functor.const 𝓘).map f
  invFun t := colimit.desc X (Cocone.mk Y t)
  left_inv f := colimit.hom_ext fun j => by simp
  right_inv t := by ext j; simp

end CategoryTheory
